def T(a, y, m, s, w, q):
    return dict(a=a, y=y, m=m, s=s, w=w, q=q)

C = {
"Post-construction duct cleaning": T(
 "Post-construction duct cleaning removes drywall dust, sawdust, debris and metal shavings from new or renovated ductwork, registers and air handler sections before the building is occupied, using source removal under negative air.",
 "Systems run during construction pull dust into ducts. When the building opens, that dust blows into finished rooms and loads new filters and coils.",
 "Our crews isolate sections, put the system under HEPA-filtered negative air and agitate debris loose, following the NADCA ACR standard as the reference for methods and cleanliness.",
 ["Linear feet of duct and number of air handlers", "Supply, return and exhaust systems included", "Access door installation responsibility", "Before and after photo documentation", "Coordination with the mechanical contractor and balancer"],
 ["Cutting access openings without the mechanical contractor's approval can void warranties.",
  "Running the HVAC system during drywall sanding undoes the cleaning.",
  "Fibrous duct liner needs gentler methods than bare sheet metal."],
 [("Do new buildings need their ducts cleaned before move-in?", "Often, yes. If the system ran during construction or ducts were left open, dust and debris are almost certainly inside, and cleaning before occupancy keeps it out of finished rooms."),
  ("What standard is used for construction duct cleaning?", "The NADCA ACR standard is the common industry reference for source removal methods and cleanliness checks, and many specifications name it directly."),
  ("How is post-construction duct cleaning actually done?", "Sections are isolated, a HEPA-filtered negative air machine pulls air through the duct, and brushes or air whips loosen debris so it is captured instead of blown into rooms."),
  ("Will you show us the ducts are clean afterward?", "Yes. Our crews take before and after photos at access points and registers so the GC and commissioning agent can see the condition of each system."),
  ("When should duct cleaning happen on a construction project?", "After dusty trades finish and before final filter change, test and balance, and occupancy. Cleaning too early means dust from finish work gets back in."),
  ("Can duct cleaning damage new ductwork?", "Not when methods match the duct type. Our crews use softer tools on lined and flexible duct and coordinate access openings with the mechanical contractor.")]),

"Ventilation commissioning cleaning": T(
 "Ventilation commissioning cleaning prepares air handlers, fans, coils, plenums and diffusers for system startup and functional testing by removing construction dust and debris that would skew readings or foul new equipment.",
 "Commissioning agents cannot test a system full of construction dust. Dirty coils and plenums reduce airflow, foul filters and can fail inspection on the first walk.",
 "Our crews clean to the commissioning agent's checklist, coordinate lockout with the mechanical contractor and document each unit before startup and test and balance.",
 ["Number of air handlers, fans and terminal units", "Commissioning checklist items assigned to cleaning", "Lockout and startup coordination", "Filter change timing", "Sign-off documentation format"],
 ["Equipment must be locked out by the mechanical contractor before any interior cleaning.",
  "Cleaning coil fins with high pressure bends them and reduces airflow.",
  "Startup before cleaning spreads construction dust through the whole building."],
 [("What gets cleaned before HVAC commissioning?", "Air handler interiors, fan sections, coils, drain pans, plenums, grilles and diffusers, plus mechanical rooms, so the equipment starts clean and test readings are accurate."),
  ("Who decides when the ventilation system is clean enough to start?", "The commissioning agent and mechanical contractor set the criteria. Our crews clean to their checklist and provide photos so they can sign off each unit."),
  ("Do you clean air handler coils as part of commissioning prep?", "Yes, with low-pressure rinsing or coil-safe cleaners where allowed, after lockout. High pressure is avoided because it bends fins and restricts airflow."),
  ("Is commissioning cleaning different from duct cleaning?", "It overlaps. Commissioning cleaning focuses on equipment and terminal devices ready for startup, while duct cleaning addresses the duct runs; many projects specify both."),
  ("Can commissioning cleaning be phased by system?", "Yes. Our crews follow the startup sequence, cleaning each air handler and its zone as the mechanical contractor releases it for commissioning."),
  ("Should filters be changed after commissioning cleaning?", "Usually. Construction filters are swapped for final filters after cleaning and before test and balance, as directed by the mechanical contractor.")]),

"Indoor air quality testing coordination": T(
 "Indoor air quality testing coordination is the preparation and scheduling work that gets a finished building ready for IAQ testing by an independent industrial hygienist or testing firm, including final cleaning, flush-out support and access.",
 "Buildings that fail an IAQ test lose days to recleaning and retesting. Good preparation means the test measures the building, not leftover construction dust.",
 "Our crews complete the cleaning the test plan requires, coordinate dates with the GC and the independent testing firm, and leave areas closed and undisturbed before sampling. We do not perform testing.",
 ["Testing firm and test plan provided by the owner or GC", "Areas and sample locations", "Flush-out or HVAC run time before testing", "Cleaning completion date before sampling", "Retest cleaning if results fall short"],
 ["Cleaning products used right before sampling can raise VOC readings.",
  "Trades working in a sampled area during testing can invalidate the results.",
  "The testing firm, not the cleaning crew, interprets and reports the results."],
 [("Does Beyond Klean perform indoor air quality testing?", "No. Testing is done by independent industrial hygienists or testing firms hired by the owner or GC. Our crews handle the cleaning and site preparation before sampling."),
  ("How do you prepare a new building for an air quality test?", "By finishing final cleaning, HEPA vacuuming dust from surfaces and grilles, using low-odor products well ahead of sampling, and keeping trades out of test areas."),
  ("Why would a new building fail an IAQ test?", "Common causes are leftover construction dust, fresh paint and adhesive vapors, and HVAC systems that were not flushed out. Cleaning and timing address the first two."),
  ("How long before IAQ sampling should cleaning be finished?", "Ideally a day or more, so dust settles and product odors clear. The testing firm's plan sets the exact window, and our crews schedule around it."),
  ("What happens if a space fails its air test?", "The testing firm reports which results fell short. Our crews reclean the affected areas as the GC directs, and the firm returns to resample."),
  ("Is IAQ testing required for LEED or similar programs?", "Some green building programs offer credits for testing or flush-out before occupancy. The owner's consultant tracks the credit; we coordinate the cleaning that supports it.")]),

"Elevator shaft and hoistway cleanup": T(
 "Elevator shaft and hoistway cleanup removes construction debris, dust, fasteners and trash from hoistway walls, sills, ledges and the pit, done under the elevator contractor's control so the elevator can pass inspection.",
 "Debris in hoistways is a fire hazard and can damage elevator equipment. Elevator inspectors routinely fail shafts and pits that still hold construction trash.",
 "Our crews work only under the elevator contractor's lockout and access control, clean ledges and sills from the top down and remove pit debris last.",
 ["Number of hoistways and stops", "Pit depth and any standing water", "Elevator contractor escort and lockout fees", "Sill and door track cleaning included", "Inspection date"],
 ["No one should enter a hoistway without the elevator contractor's access control in place.",
  "Pits may hold water, oil or debris that requires separate disposal.",
  "Cleaning the pit before the shaft above is finished wastes the effort."],
 [("Who is allowed to clean inside an elevator shaft?", "Only crews working under the elevator contractor's direction, with the car locked out and access controlled. Our crews never enter a hoistway on their own."),
  ("Why does an elevator pit need cleaning before inspection?", "Inspectors check pits for debris, water and trash because they create fire and equipment hazards. A dirty pit is a common reason elevators fail acceptance."),
  ("What is cleaned in a hoistway during construction cleanup?", "Ledges, beams, hoistway door sills and tracks, wall surfaces within reach and the pit floor, removing dust, fasteners, drywall scraps and trash."),
  ("Do I have to pay the elevator company for access?", "Usually. The elevator contractor typically charges for the mechanic who provides lockout and escort, and that cost is often separate from the cleaning price."),
  ("How long does it take to clean an elevator pit?", "A single pit is often an hour or two once access is arranged, but water, oil or heavy debris add time and may need separate disposal."),
  ("Should hoistway cleaning happen before or after cab finishes?", "Before. Shaft and pit cleanup should follow the last trade working in the hoistway and come before cab finishes and the elevator inspection.")]),

"Controlled-environment final decontamination": T(
 "Controlled-environment final decontamination is the last protocol cleaning of a cleanroom, lab, sterile suite or similar space before certification testing or use, removing particles and residues in a defined sequence with approved products.",
 "These spaces are tested against strict particle or contamination limits. A missed step or wrong product can fail certification and delay the owner's validation.",
 "This work is assigned only to trained technicians, certified where the owner requires it, who follow the owner's protocol for garments, wipes, products and sequence, top to bottom.",
 ["Owner protocol and cleanroom class or space type", "Approved products, wipes and garments", "Number of cleaning passes and sequence", "Certification or validation testing date", "Cleaning log and sign-off format"],
 ["Products not approved by the owner can leave residues that fail testing.",
  "Ordinary construction cleaning supplies shed particles inside a cleanroom.",
  "People entering after the final pass without proper gowning undo the work."],
 [("What is final decontamination in a cleanroom project?", "It is the last protocol cleaning before certification, using approved wipes, products and gowning in a set sequence to bring surfaces down to the owner's contamination limits."),
  ("Who is allowed to do controlled-environment decontamination?", "Only technicians trained in the owner's protocol, with certifications where the owner or regulations require them. We assign the work case by case to qualified crews."),
  ("Does final decontamination guarantee the room will pass certification?", "No one can guarantee a test result. Following the owner's protocol exactly gives the room the best chance, and our crews reclean if the testing firm reports issues."),
  ("How many cleaning passes does a cleanroom need before testing?", "It depends on the cleanroom class and owner protocol. Many projects call for three or more passes, moving from top to bottom and from clean areas toward the exit."),
  ("Do you supply your own cleanroom wipes and garments?", "We can, chosen to meet the owner's specification, or use the owner's supplied consumables when their protocol requires approved lots."),
  ("Do you keep records of the decontamination steps?", "Yes. Our crews log each pass, area, product and time, so the owner's validation team has a record to file with certification results.")]),

"Post-construction odor removal": T(
 "Post-construction odor removal reduces lingering smells from paint, adhesives, sealers, sawdust and new materials in a finished building through source cleaning, ventilation support and low-odor products, rather than covering them with fragrance.",
 "Strong new-construction odors bring tenant complaints and can delay move-in. Masking scents add more chemicals to the air without fixing the cause.",
 "Our crews find and clean odor sources such as adhesive smears and sealer residue, HEPA vacuum dust, and coordinate fresh air flush-out with the GC and mechanical contractor.",
 ["Rooms or floors with complaints", "Recent finish work such as paint, flooring or sealers", "HVAC flush-out availability", "Fragrance-free product requirement", "Timing before move-in or air testing"],
 ["Air fresheners and scented products mask odors and can raise VOC test results.",
  "Some odors come from curing materials that only ventilation and time will fix.",
  "Musty odors may signal moisture problems that need investigation, not cleaning."],
 [("How do you get rid of new construction smell?", "By removing sources such as adhesive and sealer residue and fine dust, then supporting fresh air flush-out. Time and ventilation finish what cleaning starts."),
  ("Do you use air fresheners to cover construction odors?", "No. Masking scents add chemicals and can affect air testing. Our crews use fragrance-free, low-odor products and focus on removing the source."),
  ("How long does paint and adhesive smell last in a new building?", "Usually days to a few weeks, depending on products used and ventilation. Running fresh air through the HVAC system shortens it considerably."),
  ("What if the smell in the new building is musty?", "A musty odor can point to trapped moisture or mold, which needs investigation by the GC or a qualified consultant before cleaning will help."),
  ("Can odor removal be done before an IAQ test?", "Yes, and it should be finished well before sampling so cleaning products have cleared and the independent testing firm measures the building itself."),
  ("Is ozone used for construction odor removal?", "Not by our crews in occupied or newly finished buildings. Ozone can harm people and some materials, so we rely on source cleaning and ventilation.")]),

"Environmental verification preparation": T(
 "Environmental verification preparation is the cleaning and readiness work done before a space is sampled, tested or inspected for dust, particles, surface residue or contamination limits, so results reflect the finished condition.",
 "A failed verification test means recleaning, retesting and schedule loss. Most failures trace back to leftover dust, recent product use or areas disturbed after cleaning.",
 "Our crews clean to the test plan's areas and limits, finish with the methods the testing firm or owner protocol specifies, then restrict access until sampling is complete.",
 ["Test type and plan from the owner or testing firm", "Areas and surfaces to be sampled", "Cleaning methods or products to avoid", "Access control after cleaning", "Retest cleaning terms"],
 ["Independent testing firms, not cleaning crews, perform and report verification tests.",
  "Any trade work in an area after final cleaning can affect sample results.",
  "Residue from the wrong cleaning product can show up in surface samples."],
 [("What does environmental verification mean on a construction project?", "It is sampling or inspection that confirms a space meets set limits for dust, particles or residues before use, performed by an independent testing firm or the owner's team."),
  ("How should a space be cleaned before surface wipe sampling?", "With the method in the test plan, usually HEPA vacuuming followed by damp wiping with approved products, working top to bottom, then keeping the area closed."),
  ("Does Beyond Klean run the verification tests?", "No. Our crews prepare the space and coordinate timing. Sampling, analysis and reporting are done by the independent testing firm or the owner's designated team."),
  ("Why keep people out of a room after pre-test cleaning?", "Foot traffic and trade work stir up and deposit dust. Restricting access until sampling is done keeps the results representative of the finished space."),
  ("What happens if verification results come back high?", "The testing firm identifies the areas involved, and our crews reclean them using the same or stricter methods before the firm resamples."),
  ("Is verification preparation the same as final cleaning?", "It builds on final cleaning, adding the specific methods, products, sequence and access control a test plan demands, and is timed tightly to the sampling date.")]),

"Precast concrete cleaning": T(
 "Precast concrete cleaning removes construction soil, mortar and sealant smears, rust stains and efflorescence from precast panels and architectural precast, using cleaners and pressures approved for the finish, after test panels confirm results.",
 "Architectural precast is a visible, expensive finish. The wrong acid or pressure can etch the surface, expose aggregate unevenly or change color permanently.",
 "Our crews follow the precast manufacturer's cleaning guidance, test a small area first, pre-wet surfaces, use the mildest effective cleaner and rinse thoroughly with low pressure.",
 ["Facade square footage and access method", "Precast finish type: smooth, sandblasted or exposed aggregate", "Stains present, such as rust, mortar or sealant", "Manufacturer cleaning guidance and test panel", "Protection of glass, metal and landscaping"],
 ["Muriatic acid can etch precast and stain adjacent glass and metal.",
  "High pressure close to the surface can scar smooth precast finishes.",
  "Sealers applied over uncleaned stains lock the stains in permanently."],
 [("How do you clean precast concrete panels after construction?", "By pre-wetting, applying a cleaner approved for that finish, gentle agitation and low-pressure rinsing, after a test area shows the method works without damage."),
  ("Can rust stains be removed from architectural precast?", "Often, with products made for rust on concrete. Our crews test first, since some rust stains go deep and can only be lightened, not fully removed."),
  ("Is acid washing safe for precast concrete?", "Only in diluted forms the manufacturer allows, on pre-wetted surfaces with thorough rinsing. Strong acid can etch the finish and damage nearby glass and metal."),
  ("Why is a test panel needed before cleaning precast?", "Precast finishes react differently to cleaners and pressure. A small test shows the result in daylight before a whole elevation is cleaned the same way."),
  ("What equipment is used to reach precast on tall buildings?", "Boom lifts, swing stages or rope access, depending on height and site. Access method is one of the biggest factors in precast cleaning price."),
  ("Should precast be cleaned before or after sealing?", "Before. Cleaning removes stains and residues first, then the sealer can be applied to a uniform surface without locking in marks.")]),

"Architectural metal cleaning": T(
 "Architectural metal cleaning removes protective film adhesive, construction dust, sealant smears, concrete splash and fingerprints from storefront frames, metal panels, anodized and painted aluminum, and stainless steel facades without scratching or dulling the finish.",
 "Metal finishes scratch, streak and discolor easily, and damage is usually permanent. Concrete splash and mortar left on aluminum can etch it within days.",
 "Our crews identify each finish, use pH-neutral cleaners and non-abrasive pads, work with the grain on stainless and rinse thoroughly, following the finish manufacturer's guidance.",
 ["Metal types and finishes: anodized, painted, stainless or copper", "Protective film removal included", "Square footage or linear feet of frames", "Access equipment needed", "Manufacturer cleaning guidance"],
 ["Abrasive pads and powdered cleansers scratch anodized and painted finishes.",
  "Protective film left in the sun too long bonds to the metal and becomes hard to remove.",
  "Alkaline masonry cleaners can etch aluminum if they splash onto frames."],
 [("How do you clean anodized aluminum storefronts after construction?", "With pH-neutral cleaner, soft pads and clean water rinses. Our crews avoid abrasives and strong alkaline or acid products that can stain or dull anodized finishes."),
  ("Can you remove old protective film adhesive from metal panels?", "Yes. Our crews use adhesive removers approved for the finish and plastic tools, testing first, because baked-on film residue needs patience to avoid scratches."),
  ("Why should stainless steel be wiped with the grain?", "Wiping across the grain leaves visible swirl marks. Working along the brushed pattern keeps the finish uniform and hides fine wiping marks."),
  ("What removes concrete splash from metal window frames?", "Gentle softening and careful scraping with plastic tools, or a cleaner approved for the metal. Acid-based concrete removers can damage aluminum and should be avoided."),
  ("Is metal facade cleaning done before final window cleaning?", "Usually at the same time or just before, since metal frames and glass are cleaned together and rinse water from one surface reaches the other."),
  ("Does architectural metal cleaning include copper and zinc?", "It can, but these metals develop natural patinas the designer may want preserved. Our crews confirm the desired appearance before any cleaning.")]),

"Sensitive historic masonry cleaning": T(
 "Sensitive historic masonry cleaning removes soiling, staining and construction residue from older brick, stone and terra cotta using the gentlest effective method, such as low-pressure water and mild cleaners, so original surfaces and mortar are preserved.",
 "Historic brick and stone are softer than modern materials. Sandblasting, high pressure or harsh acids can strip the fired face, erode mortar and cause lasting damage.",
 "Our crews follow preservation guidance, clean test patches and wait for results, then use low-pressure water, soft brushes and mild, approved cleaners, working gently from the top down.",
 ["Masonry type: brick, stone, terra cotta or stucco", "Preservation plan and reviewer approval", "Test patch locations and review period", "Water pressure and temperature limits", "Protection of windows, wood trim and landscaping"],
 ["Sandblasting and high-pressure washing permanently damage historic brick faces.",
  "Cleaning in freezing weather can trap water that cracks masonry.",
  "Some historic stone reacts badly to acid cleaners and stains permanently.",
  "Deteriorated mortar should be assessed before wet cleaning begins."],
 [("What is the safest way to clean old brick?", "Usually low-pressure water with a soft natural-bristle brush and a mild cleaner, tested on a small patch first. Gentle methods take longer but preserve the brick face."),
  ("Why not pressure wash a historic building?", "High pressure erodes the hard fired surface of old brick and washes out soft lime mortar, opening the wall to water damage that costs far more to repair."),
  ("How long should a test patch sit before full cleaning?", "Often a week or more, so the patch dries and the preservation team can judge color, residue and any damage before the method is approved for the whole wall."),
  ("Can you remove paint or graffiti from historic stone?", "Sometimes, with gentle poultices or approved removers tested first. Our crews avoid methods that would scar the stone and stop if a test shows damage."),
  ("Do historic masonry projects need approval before cleaning?", "Many do. Buildings in historic districts or with preservation funding often need the cleaning method approved by a review board or preservation consultant."),
  ("What time of year is best for historic masonry cleaning?", "Mild, dry weather. Wet cleaning should not happen when freezing is likely within a few days, because trapped water can crack brick and stone.")]),
}
